    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d193">

                <pb facs="0595" n="558" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v16/pg558"/>

                <head>193. Memorandum From the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs
                        (Brzezinski) to Secretary of State
                        Vance<anchor n="1" xml:id="d193-f1"
                    />
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1978-03-06">March 6, 1978</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Guidance on Rhodesia</item>
                </list>
                <p>As a result of our conversation with the President today, the following decisions
                    on Rhodesia were taken:<anchor n="2" xml:id="d193-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>1. The United States should seek the convening of a conference of the
                    nationalists, the front-line countries, and—if possible—Smith to try to bring agreement among them on
                    the basis of the Anglo-American plan, incorporating the positive features of the
                    Salisbury settlement.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d193-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>2. We will not support the Salisbury settlements nor condemn them. We will remain
                    committed to the Anglo-American plan, and seek to bring the Salisbury settlement
                    into conformity with it.</p>
                <p>3. We will not support a veto by the British of the Nigerian Resolution.<anchor
                        n="4" xml:id="d193-f4"/> We will abstain with an explanation of our vote.
                    However, we should also work to develop a resolution which all can support.</p>
                <p>4. We should go forward with planning for a May visit from President Kaunda of Zambia.</p>
                <pb facs="0596" n="559" xml:id="pg559"/>
                <p>The Department of State should prepare a country paper on Zambia, outlining its
                    economic and military needs and the extent to which U.S. assistance might be made available.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">Zbigniew
                            Brzezinski</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d193-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d193-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Carter Library, National Security Affairs, Brzezinski Material; Country
                    File, Box 88, Zimbabwe (Rhodesia): 1–4/78. Secret; Sensitive. </p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d193-f2" n="2" xml:id="d193-f2-content">
                <p>According to the President’s Daily Diary, Carter met with Mondale,
                        Vance, Young, Brzezinski,
                        Aaron, and Jordan from 2 to 3 p.m in
                    the Oval Office. (Carter Library, Presidential Materials) </p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d193-f3" n="3" xml:id="d193-f3-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d192">Document 192</ref>. </p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d193-f4" n="4" xml:id="d193-f4-content">
                <p>Reference is presumably to UNSC Resolution 423
                    (1978), adopted March 14, by a vote of 10 to 0 with 5 abstentions (including the
                    United Kingdom, the United States, France, Germany, and Canada). (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Yearbook of the United Nations, 1978</hi>, p. 227)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
